**Proof.**

Let $\omega_1$ be the circumcircle of $\triangle PRS$ and $\omega_2$ the circumcircle of $\triangle QRS$.

**Claim 1.** $AB$ is tangent to $\omega_1$ at $P$.

Since $P\in\overline{AB}$ and $S\in\overline{BC}$ with $S$ between $B$ and $R$, the line $PS$ cuts off the corner $B$ of $\triangle ABC$; hence $B$ and $R$ lie on opposite sides of line $PS$. By the tangent–chord angle theorem, the angle between the tangent to $\omega_1$ at $P$ (taken on the side of $B$) and the chord $PS$ equals the inscribed angle $\angle PRS$ subtended by $PS$ on the opposite side (where $R$ lies). The hypothesis $\angle BPS=\angle PRS$ therefore identifies the ray $PB$—and hence the line $AB$—as the tangent to $\omega_1$ at $P$.

**Claim 2.** $AC$ is tangent to $\omega_2$ at $Q$.

Likewise $Q\in\overline{AC}$ and $R\in\overline{BC}$; the line $QR$ cuts off the corner $C$, so $C$ and $S$ lie on opposite sides of line $QR$ (since $S$ lies between $B$ and $R$, on the side away from $C$). The tangent–chord theorem gives that the angle between the tangent to $\omega_2$ at $Q$ (on the side of $C$) and chord $QR$ equals $\angle QSR$. The hypothesis $\angle CQR=\angle QSR$ identifies the ray $QC$—hence the line $AC$—as the tangent to $\omega_2$ at $Q$.

**Powers of $A$.** Since $AB$ is tangent to $\omega_1$ at $P$ and $A\in AB$ with $A\neq P$, the point $A$ is exterior to $\omega_1$ and
$$\operatorname{Pow}_{\omega_1}(A)=AP^{2}.$$
Similarly, since $AC$ is tangent to $\omega_2$ at $Q$,
$$\operatorname{Pow}_{\omega_2}(A)=AQ^{2}.$$
The given condition $AP=AQ$ yields
$$\operatorname{Pow}_{\omega_1}(A)=\operatorname{Pow}_{\omega_2}(A),$$
so $A$ lies on the radical axis of $\omega_1$ and $\omega_2$.

**Conclusion.** Both $\omega_1$ and $\omega_2$ pass through the two distinct points $R$ and $S$. If $\omega_1\neq\omega_2$, their radical axis is the line $RS$, which is the line $BC$. But $A$ is a vertex of the nondegenerate triangle $ABC$, so $A\notin BC$—contradicting the fact that $A$ lies on the radical axis. Hence $\omega_1=\omega_2$.

Therefore $P,Q,R,S$ all lie on the single circle $\omega_1=\omega_2$; i.e. $P,Q,R,S$ are concyclic. $\blacksquare$

